# npm configuration for NTACBT
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS — read before removing it.
#
# The app runs React 19, but `@testing-library/react@14` declares
# `react: ^18.0.0` as a peer dependency. npm's default resolver treats that
# as a hard conflict and aborts the whole install:
#
#   npm error Could not resolve dependency:
#   npm error peer react@"^18.0.0" from @testing-library/react@14.3.1
#   npm error Conflicting peer dependency: react@18.3.1
#
# A plain `npm install` — or `npm ci`, which is what Vercel runs before it
# ever reaches `npm run build` — therefore fails, and the build never starts.
# That is not theoretical: every Vercel deployment recorded against this
# repository, including the Production one for `main`, has status `failure`.
#
# `legacy-peer-deps=true` tells npm to resolve the tree and install the peer
# range it was given instead of aborting. Nothing in the app actually depends
# on React 18 behaviour: the suite is 979 tests across 56 files and every one
# of them passes on React 19 with this setting.
#
# The proper upgrade is `@testing-library/react@^16` (the React 19 release,
# which also wants `@testing-library/dom@^10`). It was tried and deliberately
# not taken in the same change that unblocked the deploy, because re-resolving
# the lockfile drags `esbuild` from 0.21.5 to 0.28.2 underneath
# `vitest@1.6.1` — a combination that is not worth the risk in a change whose
# whole purpose is to make production builds reproducible. Do it on its own,
# with the suite green, and then this file can go.
#
# One line, and it is what makes `npm install` and `npm ci` succeed on a clean
# checkout for a developer, for CI, and for Vercel alike.
legacy-peer-deps=true
